WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:07.470
On July 13, 1944, William James Sidis’s
landlord found him unconscious in his small

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.230
Boston boarding house.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.300
He had suffered a massive stroke.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.210
And lay dying.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:16.279
William may have been the smartest person
who ever lived.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:20.520
His IQ was somewhere between 250 and 300.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.060
To put that in perspective, Einstein’s was
estimated to be 200.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.670
William’s parents were brilliant Ukrainian
Jewish immigrants.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:33.800
Boris Sidis was a renowned psychologist, Sarah
Sidis went to medical school at a time when

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.410
few women did.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:40.110
William - or Billy as he was known - learned
the alphabet by six months of age.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.860
At 18 months, he could read the New York Times.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.200
He enrolled at Harvard University at age 11!

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.980
When he graduated, William told reporters
he intended to live the “perfect life”.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.710
But that would not come to be.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:59.760
William James Sidis was born on April Fool’s
Day, 1898, in New York City.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.610
Violent anti-Semitic attacks in Ukraine, which
was then part of the Russian empire, drove

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.740
his parents to the promised land, where they
met when Boris tutored Sarah in English.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.740
She encouraged him to go to Harvard, where
he studied psychology and used some of his

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.260
psychological principles to help mold his
son.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.950
He said: “You know the old saying - ‘As
the twig is bent, the tree’s inclined.’

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.060
Parents cannot too soon begin the work of
bending the minds of their children in the

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.660
right direction…”

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:33.980
William’s parents attributed his extraordinary
intelligence not to genes but to his upbringing.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.560
As a baby, they treated him like an adult.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.049
When he was a few months old, William’s
parents placed food and a spoon in front of

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:42.049
him.

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:44.830
They let William observe, and he learned to
feed himself.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.630
His mum recalled: “He squealed with delight.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.399
No one had taught him; he had reasoned it
out.”

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:55.000
This natural way of reasoning is also how
his parents taught him how to spell.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Instead of learning through memorization,
William learned to spell by focusing on the

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.710
underlying patterns and structures.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.940
For example, if he learned the word “BAT”
using letter blocks, then he understood that

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.500
modifying the word by, say, swapping out the
“a” for an “e” would form a new word

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.300
with a different meaning.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.280
In the same way that he learned to spell,
William learned eight languages by the age

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.590
of eight by understanding the fundamental
principles of language structure.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:28.670
Besides English, he knew: Latin, Greek, German,
Russian, Hebrew, Turkish, French, and Armenian.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:34.810
He even invented his own language as a child,
Vendergood which drew on European influences.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.910
Instead of reading him fairy tales at bedtime,
his parents read him Greek myths, which helped

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.890
him learn about the planets.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:47.890
Jupiter is named after the Roman god Jupiter
which is adapted from Zeus in Greek mythology.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.819
Every moment was an opportunity to learn.

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:54.430
William had no time for or interest in playing
with toys.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.360
He didn’t play sports or any games at recess.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.790
His father thought such childish pursuits
were silly and meaningless.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:07.060
For some reason, he had a tremendous fear
of dogs.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:12.030
His only enjoyment was riding on streetcars
when his parents took him to museums, libraries,

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.010
parks, and zoos.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.430
He collected 1,600 streetcar transfers by
the time he was in his twenties.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.790
William ended up finishing elementary school
in less than one year, and high school in

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.470
six weeks.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.670
His mom remarked: “The newspapers never
missed a chance to try and prove that he was

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.980
insane, or psychotic, or simply a freak.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.269
In truth, Billy was a completely normal child
in every respect.”

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:40.459
The media attention followed him to Harvard.

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:44.739
Where they portrayed him as a “know it all”,
like in this New Yorker cartoon where he’s

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:46.970
lecturing to a group of men.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:51.519
After entering Harvard at age 11, he gave
a lecture to the Math Club about the fourth

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:55.440
dimension, which is an extension beyond the
3D space we experience.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.629
Here, he’s talking about shapes with many
sides in the fourth dimension, which demonstrates

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:06.420
his astounding ability to comprehend complex
mathematical concepts beyond his years.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.599
A professor at MIT who attended the lecture
proclaimed: “I predict that young Sidis

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:13.920
will be a great astronomical mathematician.”

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.699
Yet, this never happened.

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:19.859
In fact, he would grow to resent mathematics.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:23.870
Shortly after the lecture, he fell ill, which
made all the news.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:28.100
The media reported that he had suffered a
mental breakdown which was a rumor that dogged

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.180
him his entire life.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.260
He did live with his family on an estate in
New Hampshire that was also the grounds of

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.220
the sanatorium Boris was running.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.220
(Sidis Psychotherapeutic Institute)

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.020
William only returned to Harvard several months
later and never fit in.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:46.470
His biographer Amy Wallace described how he
was: “A complete freak in the eyes of his

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.710
fellow students, he had none of the social
graces, no interest in sports or girls, and

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.699
was several years younger.”

00:04:53.699 --> 00:04:58.560
When he graduated from Harvard, he told reporters,
"I want to live the perfect life.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.949
The only way to live the perfect life is to
live it in seclusion.

00:05:01.949 --> 00:05:04.370
I have always hated crowds.”

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:10.540
His idea of the perfect life is described
in a paper he wrote utopian society called

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.540
Hesperia.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:17.699
Hesperia’s Constitution begins with: “We,
the people”, reminiscent of the U.S. Constitution.

00:05:17.699 --> 00:05:20.070
But that was where the similarities end.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:24.669
In Hesperia, those who want citizenship have
to pass an intelligence test.

00:05:24.669 --> 00:05:29.030
Marriage is forbidden, while polygamy is completely
legal.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:34.280
Interestingly, children are not raised by
their biological parents, and instead, “Male

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.750
children shall be assigned to the charge of
male guardians, and female children to the

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.069
charge of female guardians.”

00:05:41.069 --> 00:05:47.160
He rejected the influence of his parents,
particularly that of his domineering mother.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.790
After leaving Harvard, he took up a position
teaching mathematics at Rice Institute, now

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:54.030
Rice University in Houston.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.759
He was 17 years old.

00:05:55.759 --> 00:05:59.250
Just like at Harvard, William didn’t fit
in at Rice.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.880
Students teased him for never having kissed
a girl.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:04.410
Girls faked crushes on him as a joke.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:08.530
He stayed at Rice for only eight months and
then enrolled at Harvard Law School in the

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.180
fall of 1916.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:15.070
Then, for some unknown reason, he quit in
his last semester and failed to earn a law

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.070
degree.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:19.280
His mother was furious and would tell people
that her son left his studies because Harvard

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.729
Law had to shut down due to World War I, which
was not true.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:25.810
William was very much anti-war.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.440
He refused to register for the draft and nearly
went to prison if it weren’t for the armistice

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.580
of 1918 that ended the first world war.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.470
He was extremely passionate about politics.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:41.449
He was briefly jailed after leading a May
Day rally in Boston, an anti-war, workers’

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:44.200
rights rally organized by the Socialists that
turned violent.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.200
organized by the Socialists, organized by
the Socialists, which also conveyed a strong

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:46.200
anti-war message.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.160
He rebelled against the capitalist system
that propelled his parents from their status

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:54.319
as poor immigrants to a position of success
in American society.

00:06:54.319 --> 00:06:59.600
While in jail, he met and fell head over heels
for Martha Foley, a 20-year-old Irish girl

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:00.600
who grew up in Boston.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:01.600
“HARVARD’S BOY PRODIGY VOWS NEVER TO MARRY
SIDIS PLEDGES CELIBACY BENEATH STURDY OAK,

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:02.600
HAS 154 RULES WHICH GOVERN HIS LIFE, ‘WOMEN
DO NOT APPEAL TO ME,’ HE SAYS; HE IS 16.”

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.500
Despite telling the Boston Herald earlier
that he vowed to remain celibate, saying,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.009
“Women do not appeal to me.”

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:10.690
After the arrest, he followed Martha to New
York.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.620
They kissed, but Martha assured him there
would be nothing more.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:20.000
William’s unrequited love may have affected
the trajectory of his life.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.630
William's friend Julius Eichel said: "Sidis
admitted that her love might have achieved

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.470
wonders with him…"

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:30.970
He carried a photograph of her in his pocket
until the day he died.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:36.069
Martha went on to marry a writer and achieved
notoriety for co-founding the literary magazine

00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:37.129
Story.

00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:41.629
When her memoirs were published, she briefly
mentioned William in a single line, in which

00:07:41.629 --> 00:07:47.349
she called him “the famous and tragic prodigy
who was the first boy ever to pay court to

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:48.349
me.”

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:49.349
(72%)

00:07:49.349 --> 00:07:52.310
Despite his brilliance, William failed to
achieve greatness.

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:55.750
And any work that did show promise was overlooked.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:01.539
In 1925, he published a book that challenged
the Second Law of Thermodynamics, the principle

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:06.389
that the universe is headed toward “heat
death” where all energy is evenly spread

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:10.440
out, so there won’t be any energy left to
power any processes.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.930
In this state, nothing will move, change,
or live.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:17.500
(frozen tableau imagery)
In The Animate and the Inanimate, he explored

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.570
the possibility of reversing the universe’s
direction.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:26.229
He theorized that living things could tap
into a hidden source of energy to counteract

00:08:26.229 --> 00:08:27.229
the process.

00:08:27.229 --> 00:08:31.889
He gave an example of a ball bouncing down
the stairs, which, when played in reverse

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:36.789
on film, appears as if a hidden source of
energy is pushing the ball up the stairs.

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:41.570
He did acknowledge that his work was speculative
and his theory has never been proven.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:44.440
But it was never taken seriously in the first
place.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:45.450
It was ignored.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:49.180
There wasn’t a single review during its
time.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:54.120
He made headlines for being a boy prodigy
and for his private life, yet no one paid

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.940
attention to his academic work.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.600
He stopped writing about physics, math, and
cosmology altogether.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.540
While in New York, he took on menial jobs,
including working as an operator in an office

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:10.970
that was using an early mechanical calculator
called a comptometer.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:16.300
The papers were all too keen to splash headlines
about the boy wonder earning an average wage

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.880
of $23 bucks a week.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.310
He hid his genius from his co-workers.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.720
When they found out who he was, he’d move
on to the next job.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.620
He refused to accept higher-paying work in
which he had to use his intellect.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:32.579
Once, he was offered a job with a railway
company that tried to put him to work solving

00:09:32.579 --> 00:09:33.790
their technical problems.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:37.529
One of the officials later found him crying.

00:09:37.529 --> 00:09:41.110
William told him he couldn’t bear the thought
of doing math.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:45.540
Another time, a cousin needed his math skills
to help solve a dental problem involving the

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.100
alignment of teeth.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:51.390
He offered William $3,000, the equivalent
of $55,000 today.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.360
It would have taken him two hours to solve.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.540
But he refused.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:10:00.630
William’s biographer concludes: “...he
could not take a job of complex figuring without

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:04.000
risking emotional and physical illness.”

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.590
His entire life had been defined by pressure
to perform at a high level.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.850
At the expense of mastering basic life skills.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.540
He confessed to an aunt that he had never
been taught to tie his shoelaces.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:19.700
His parents may have provided him with an
exceptional education, yet they failed to

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.290
teach him the basics of grooming.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:25.710
He didn’t shave regularly, wore a worn-out
cap.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:31.420
He reportedly didn’t bathe, or at least
not often, and the stench was brutal.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:37.510
When his father suffered a stroke and died
in October 1923, he never attended the funeral.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:42.650
According to William’s biographer, the reason
was that he refused to see his mum.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.100
William hated the way she dominated him as
a child.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.100
They never reconciled.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.050
The Sidis’ didn’t raise their other child
with the same intensity.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:54.540
William’s younger sister Helena did not
get a formal elementary or high school education.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.650
Helena said that her dad had become so fed
up with William that he didn’t want to educate

00:10:58.650 --> 00:10:59.650
her.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.060
She was also seriously ill as a child.

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:06.070
Nevertheless, she thrived under the guidance
of her brother, who taught her how to read

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:10.170
and write, and she passed the entrance exams
to attend university.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:15.060
In his thirties, William settled in Boston,
again, doing menial jobs.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:19.779
The media had a hay day when they found out
he was working as a lowly office clerk.

00:11:19.779 --> 00:11:23.310
An unflattering article in a Boston daily
newspaper read:

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:28.760
“William J. Sidis, now thirty-nine, was
once declared by a group of eminent scientists

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:34.149
to be a coming innovator in the field of science,
with potentialities as great as Einstein and

00:11:34.149 --> 00:11:36.399
as brilliant as Marconi.

00:11:36.399 --> 00:11:41.700
Yet yesterday a Sunday Advertiser writer found
him in a small room, wall-papered and dark,

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:49.120
where for the past five years he has lived
unknown, unsung, uncaring.”

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.620
His health had also started to decline.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:56.170
He was overweight and had high blood pressure,
which eventually caused a stroke.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.889
The kind that claimed his father.

00:11:58.889 --> 00:12:06.700
On July 17, 1944, William James Sidis died
of a cerebral hemorrhage which led to pneumonia.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.000
He was 46 years old.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.830
He was buried beside his father in New Hampshire.

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:20.980
Did William Sidis’ parents push him too
far?

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.370
A faculty member at Rice University, where
William briefly taught, disagreed.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:30.540
Dr. Guérard believes: “He was the victim
not of intensive education given him by his

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:37.630
father, nor of the romantic curse called Genius,
but of the thoughtless cruelty of the public.”

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:42.410
The New York Times once excitedly described
William James Sidis as a "wonderfully successful

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:48.360
result of a scientific forcing experiment,
and as such furnishes one of the most interesting

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.320
mental phenomena in history."

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:58.940
As we now know, early success did not guarantee
a fulfilling and successful life.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:03.440
If William had experienced a different upbringing,
the outcome of his life might have been entirely

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:04.440
different.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:08.640
One thing that's certain is his exceptional
mastery of science and mathematics.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.880
If you’d like to sharpen your skills in
STEM, I recommend Brilliant.org, a website,

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:16.910
